Silojack

Ingredients: CHICAGO HOUSE & GARAGE HOUSE + INDUSTRIAL TECHNO & SCHRANZ + REBEL, ANGRY & AGGRESSIVE + Synth + 1970s ERA
Creative Catalyst: The movement started in a seed vault, sung to keep the archive company.

Silojack is a feral collision of underground sanctuary and violent acceleration: the swinging, syncopated shuffle and soulful organ stabs of late-night garage house are fed into the bone-cracking, 160-BPM overdriven kick barrages of Schranz, all driven by freezing, pitch-drifting 1970s monophonic synthesizers and raw, defiant human howls.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 155–165 BPM. A relentless, four-on-the-floor Schranz kick drum distorted into a square wave, violently counterbalanced by an aggressive, loose-pocket garage swing on the off-beat open hi-hats and snare skanks.
- Harmonic Palette: Gospel-rooted minor 9th and suspended 4th chords transposed into severe, cold Dorian and Phrygian modes; warm 1970s chord progressions are violently pitch-bent and detuned by sub-zero analog oscillator drift.
- Production Signatures:
    1. Frost-Line Saturation: Late-1970s reel-to-reel tape recorders ran intentionally below freezing temperatures, causing condensation across the tape heads that compresses low-end sub-bass into an abrasive, paper-tearing crunch.
    2. Husk-Gated Room Mics: Sidechaining room ambient microphones through acoustic agricultural grain sorters, turning the clatter of cascading dried seeds into violent, rhythmic white-noise gates.
    3. The Permafrost Belt: Soulful garage house vocal hooks belted without pitch correction into high-gain guitar amplifiers, creating a furious, reverbless bark that clips against the ceiling of the mix.
- Dynamics: Suffocating density alternating with stark, sudden vacuums. Wall-of-sound industrial percussion will cut out instantly, leaving only a shivering 1970s synth lead and an unhinged, solo vocal cry before collapsing back into full-throttle 160-BPM assault without any buildup or riser.

Scene & Origin Lore
Silojack was born four hundred feet beneath the permafrost in the decommissioned access tunnels flanking the Svalbard Global Seed Vault during the brutal polar night of 2024. A rogue contingent of renegade botanists, night-shift refrigeration mechanics, and displaced club kids grew terrified that the sub-zero silence was sterilizing the spirits of the slumbering agricultural specimens. Defying facility protocol, they smuggled in a water-damaged 1974 Minimoog, a dual-deck tape console, and an array of rusted industrial solenoids, rigging them to produce seismic jack-tracks to "keep the barley alive." Silojack fashion is fiercely utilitarian and anti-sterile: oversized vulcanized rubber archive aprons worn over grease-stained 1970s northern soul track jackets, balaclavas lined with uncarded raw sheep wool, and safety lanyards braided with dehydrated heirloom pea pods. Gatherings—known as "Germination Raves"—take place inside abandoned permafrost shafts where participants perform the "Tillage Stomp," a furious heel-first step that pounds warmth into the concrete while singing defiant house chants into exhaust vents.

Central Instruments:
- 1974 Minimoog Model D (pushed into self-oscillating, screaming resonance)
- EMS VCS3 "Put-Together" synth (used for microtonal wind and valve-leak FX)
- Modified 1970s Hammond B3 drawbar organ routed through an industrial fuzz pedal
- Rusted grain-hopper metal percussions and pneumatic air-valves
- Roland TR-909 run through an overdriven 1976 Otari MX-5050 tape deck
- Raw, unamplified, overdriven human gospel/rebel screams

